            (see there or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, example 5.5.6.13
          %p
            Moreover, every morphism is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-2%29-connected"} (-2)-connected
          %p
            Therefore for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = -2
            the
            = succeed "-connected/" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-truncated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h3#the_case__2
            The case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = -1
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncated"} (-1)-truncated
            morphism is precisely a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} full and faithful morphism
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-connected"} (-1)-connected
            morphism is one whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited"} inhabited
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            a morphism between
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                is full and faithful precisely if it is an
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            %li
              %p
                has non-empty fibers precisely if it is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
          %p
            Therefore between 0-truncated objects the (-1)-connected/(-1)-truncated factorization system is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epi%2Fmono+factorization+system"} epi/mono factorization system
            and hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            factorization.
          %p
            Generally, the (-1)-connected/(-1)-truncated factorization is through the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-categorical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-image"} 1-image
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+image"} homotopy image
            (see there for more details).
          %h3#the_case__3
            The case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y
            be two
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy 1-types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
          %p
            A morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
            precisely if it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
          %p
            A morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-connected"} 0-connected
            precisely if it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full functor
            and an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+and+full+functor"} essentially surjective and full functor
          %p
            Therefore on homotopy 1-types the 0-connected/0-truncated factorization system is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28eso%2Bfull%2C+faithful%29+factorization+system"} (eso+full, faithful) factorization system
          %h2#references References
          %p The general abstract statement is in
          %ul
            %li#Lurie
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-theoretic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
          %ul
            %li#Rezk
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"}> Charles Rezk
              ,
              %em Toposes and homotopy toposes
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/~rezk/homotopy-topos-sketch.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"}> Univalent Foundations Project
              , section 7.6 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
        .revisedby
